          "number": 6,
          "letter": "F",
          "english": "Perfectionism can, of course, be a positive force. Think of professional athletes, who train aggressively for ever-higher levels of competition. In well-adjusted perfectionism, someone who doesn't get the gold is able to forget the setback and move on. In maladaptive(不当的) perfectionism, meanwhile, people make an archive of all their failures. They revisit these archives constantly, thinking, as Pryor puts it, \"I need to make myself feel terrible so I don't do this again.\" Then they double down, \"raising the expectation bar even higher, which increases the likelihood of defeat, which makes you self-critical, so you raise the bar higher, work even harder,\" she says. Next comes failure, shame, and pushing yourself even harder toward even higher and more impossible goals. Meeting them becomes an \"all or nothing\" premise. Pryor offered this example: \"Even if I'm an incredible attorney, if I don't make partner in the same pacing as one of my colleagues, clearly that means I'm a failure.\"",
          "chinese": "当然，完美主义也可以是一种积极的力量。想想职业运动员吧，他们刻苦训练，以应对越来越高水平的竞争。在适应良好的完美主义中，一个没有获得金牌的人能够忘记这次挫折，继续前进。而在适应不良的完美主义中，人们会把自己所有的失败都整理成档案。他们不断回顾这些档案，像普赖尔所说的那样想着：“我需要让自己感觉很糟糕，这样我就不会再犯同样的错误。”然后他们会变本加厉，“把期望的标准提高得更高，这增加了失败的可能性，使你开始自我批评，于是你又把标准提高，工作更加努力，”她说。接下来便是失败、羞耻，以及把自己推向更高、更不可能实现的目标。达到这些目标成了一个“非此即彼”的前提。普赖尔举例说：“即使我是一个出色的律师，如果我没有和某位同事以同样的速度成为合伙人，那显然就意味着我是个失败者。”",
